1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,028
How does a vendor app control a device?

2
00:00:02,048 --> 00:00:04,725
Follow the evidence back.

3
00:00:04,745 --> 00:00:06,424
It is REA,

4
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:09,328
Reverse Engineer Anything,

5
00:00:09,348 --> 00:00:10,793
released under the MIT license.

6
00:00:10,813 --> 00:00:12,684
As of today,

7
00:00:12,704 --> 00:00:15,709
it has about 218 thousand GitHub stars.

8
00:00:15,729 --> 00:00:19,003
It connects to an agent, then inspects web apps,

9
00:00:19,023 --> 00:00:21,940
binaries, and runtime behavior.

10
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:26,109
ADB can find and pull APKs; network traffic

11
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:30,663
can come from HAR or mitmproxy captures.

12
00:00:30,683 --> 00:00:34,180
Firmware can go through Binwalk and Unblob,

13
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,244
then selected native files into Ghidra.

14
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:40,295
Evidence and limitations return to Claude Code

15
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:43,747
or Codex before the agent reconstructs a feature.

16
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:47,003
You may build your own controller

17
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:48,956
for an AC, heater,

18
00:00:48,976 --> 00:00:52,178
or light—but protocol and access decide.

19
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:54,093
The vendor app is only an entry point;

20
00:00:54,113 --> 00:00:57,985
the device's real rules can finally be seen.
